            "Antenatal care is the most-used health service in Ghana — about 98% of women attend at least once — and the one with the biggest gap between attendance and content: a card showing eight stamps but no blood pressure or urine test is a record of visits, not of care. The first contact should be <strong>by 12 weeks</strong>, when dating is most accurate, when anaemia, syphilis, HIV, hypertension and sickle cell disease can be found before they do damage, when folic acid still matters, when iron and malaria prevention can start, and when an unwanted pregnancy can still be discussed lawfully; the woman who books at 30 weeks has lost all of this. The pregnancy is confirmed by a <strong>urine hCG test</strong> (positive from around the missed period), by the fetal heart on Doppler from 10–12 weeks or by ultrasound, and dated by <strong>Naegele's rule</strong> — first day of the last normal period <strong>+ 7 days − 3 months + 1 year</strong> (LMP 12 March 2026 gives an EDD of 19 December 2026), adjusting for cycles longer or shorter than 28 days, or by an ultrasound before 14 weeks when the dates are uncertain, the cycles irregular or the woman was breastfeeding or using hormonal contraception. <strong>Gestation</strong> is counted in completed weeks from the LMP; <strong>term</strong> is 37 to 41 completed weeks; a woman who is unsure of her dates is dated by fundal height and the earliest scan available.",
            "<strong>Gravidity</strong> is the number of pregnancies including this one, whatever the outcome; <strong>parity</strong> is the number of births after 24 (in some texts 28) weeks, live or still, counting twins as one birth. A woman in her fourth pregnancy with two live children and one miscarriage is gravida 4 para 2 (+1); a <strong>primigravida</strong> is in her first pregnancy, a <strong>multigravida</strong> has been pregnant before, and a <strong>grand multipara</strong> has had five or more births — a risk group for malpresentation, postpartum haemorrhage and uterine rupture. The <strong>booking history</strong> covers the present pregnancy (LMP, symptoms, bleeding, planned or not, feelings about it), every previous pregnancy in order (year, gestation, mode of birth, weight, complications such as haemorrhage, pre-eclampsia, caesarean section or stillbirth, and whether the child is alive), medical history (hypertension, diabetes, sickle cell disease, asthma, epilepsy, heart disease, tuberculosis, HIV, mental illness, surgery, transfusions, allergies and drugs including herbal preparations), family history (twins, diabetes, hypertension, sickle cell, congenital anomalies), and social history — age, education, occupation, partner, who makes decisions, money, distance from the facility, substance use and whether she is safe at home. The <strong>examination</strong> records height (under about 150 cm is a risk for disproportion), weight and BMI, blood pressure (the booking reading is the baseline every later reading is judged against), pallor, jaundice, oedema, thyroid, heart and lungs, breasts, the abdomen (scars, fundal height) and, when indicated, a speculum or pelvic examination.",
