            "<strong>Malaria in pregnancy</strong> is often asymptomatic in the semi-immune Ghanaian woman and still causes maternal anaemia, placental infection, low birth weight, preterm birth and stillbirth. Three measures protect her: <strong>intermittent preventive treatment with sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine (IPTp-SP)</strong> — three tablets as a single dose at every scheduled contact <strong>from 16 weeks</strong> (or from quickening in the second trimester), doses at least one month apart, aiming for <strong>at least three and up to five doses</strong>, <strong>given under direct observation</strong> at the clinic with water so that it is actually taken; sleeping under an <strong>insecticide-treated net</strong> every night from the first contact (nets are issued free at booking); and <strong>prompt testing and treatment of fever</strong>. SP is not given in the first trimester (folate antagonism during organogenesis), is not given to women taking cotrimoxazole prophylaxis for HIV (the combination duplicates the sulphonamide and the cotrimoxazole already protects), and is not given within a month of the previous dose. Daily folic acid at 0.4 mg does not interfere with SP, but the 5 mg folic acid tablet does and should not be given on the same day. Women with sickle cell disease receive the same IPTp-SP and need their folic acid continued.",
            "<strong>Tetanus</strong> in the newborn and the mother is prevented by the tetanus–diphtheria (Td) vaccine, a five-dose lifetime schedule: a woman never vaccinated receives dose 1 at the first contact, dose 2 at least four weeks later (the two doses in pregnancy give the baby protection if the second is at least two weeks before birth), dose 3 six months after that, and doses 4 and 5 at yearly intervals in subsequent pregnancies — after which she is protected for life and her card says so. <strong>Iron and folic acid</strong> — in Ghana usually ferrous sulphate 200 mg (65 mg elemental iron) with folic acid daily, or a combined tablet of 60 mg elemental iron and 400 micrograms folic acid — are given to every pregnant woman from booking through the postnatal period because the demands of pregnancy cannot be met from the diet; the woman is told to expect black stools and possibly constipation or nausea, to take the tablet with food or at night if it upsets her, to take it with orange or other vitamin C-rich food rather than tea or milk, and to keep it away from children. Treatment doses are higher when she is anaemic. <strong>Deworming</strong> with a single dose of mebendazole 500 mg or albendazole 400 mg after the first trimester removes the hookworm that competes with the iron tablet. <strong>Nutrition</strong> counselling is practical: an extra meal a day, locally available iron-rich foods (dark green leaves such as kontomire, beans, groundnuts, liver, fish including small whole fish, eggs), iodised salt, fruit, enough water, and no 'eating for two' of starch alone; food taboos (eggs, snails and some fish are forbidden to pregnant women in some communities) are discussed respectfully and replaced with what the family can accept. Calcium supplements (1.5–2 g daily) are recommended where dietary calcium is low to reduce pre-eclampsia, and low-dose aspirin from 12 weeks is given to women at high risk of pre-eclampsia.",
