          heading: "The hypertensive disorders: a disease of the whole body, not the blood pressure",
          paragraphs: [
            "Hypertensive disorders complicate about one pregnancy in ten and are, with haemorrhage, the leading cause of maternal death in Ghana's hospitals. The classification is simple. <strong>Chronic hypertension</strong> is high blood pressure before 20 weeks or before pregnancy. <strong>Gestational hypertension</strong> is a new blood pressure of <strong>140/90 mmHg or more on two readings at least four hours apart after 20 weeks</strong>, without proteinuria — a woman at 34 weeks with 152/98 on two readings four hours apart and no protein; it needs closer surveillance because a quarter progress to pre-eclampsia. <strong>Pre-eclampsia</strong> is gestational hypertension <strong>with proteinuria</strong> (1+ or more on dipstick, confirmed) or with evidence of organ involvement (low platelets, raised creatinine or liver enzymes, pulmonary oedema, headache or visual symptoms). It is a disorder of the placenta and of the mother's blood vessels — widespread endothelial damage, vasospasm, leaky capillaries and activated clotting — so the blood pressure is only the sign that is easiest to measure; the kidneys, liver, brain, lungs, platelets and placenta are all involved, and <strong>delivery of the placenta is the only cure</strong>. Everything else buys time safely. <strong>Severe pre-eclampsia</strong> is blood pressure of <strong>160/110 or more</strong>, or any pressure with severe features: a <strong>headache that paracetamol does not relieve</strong>, <strong>blurred vision or flashing lights</strong>, <strong>epigastric or right upper quadrant pain</strong> (the liver capsule stretching), nausea and vomiting, brisk reflexes with clonus, oliguria (under 30 mL an hour or under 400 mL a day), pulmonary oedema, platelets under 100, rising creatinine or transaminases, or fetal growth restriction. <strong>HELLP syndrome</strong> — Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets — is a severe variant that can present with epigastric pain and malaise and a near-normal blood pressure. <strong>Eclampsia</strong> is a generalised tonic-clonic fit in a woman with pre-eclampsia, before, during or up to several weeks after birth; a third of fits occur postpartum, which is why the blood pressure is checked in the puerperium.",
            "Risk factors are a first pregnancy or a new partner, a previous or family history of pre-eclampsia, age under 18 or over 35, chronic hypertension, diabetes, renal disease, lupus, obesity, multiple pregnancy, molar pregnancy and a long interval since the last birth. <strong>Prevention</strong> for women at high risk is <strong>low-dose aspirin (75–150 mg at night) from 12 weeks</strong> until birth and <strong>calcium 1.5–2 g daily</strong> where dietary intake is low. <strong>Detection</strong> is the blood pressure and urine dipstick at every antenatal contact — a woman at 34 weeks with a pressure of 158/104 and protein 3+ has severe pre-eclampsia whatever she says she feels, and a card with eight visits but no blood pressure recorded has screened for nothing. Oedema is no longer a diagnostic criterion (most pregnant women have ankle swelling), but sudden swelling of the face and hands with a weight gain of more than a kilogram in a week is a warning.",
            "<strong>Management of pre-eclampsia without severe features</strong>: review twice weekly or admit, blood pressure and urine, bloods (full blood count, creatinine, urate, liver enzymes), fetal growth and movements, oral antihypertensive if the pressure is persistently 150/100 or more, and <strong>birth at 37 weeks</strong>. <strong>Severe pre-eclampsia</strong> is admitted to a facility with a doctor, theatre and blood, and treated on three fronts at once: <strong>control the blood pressure</strong> (target 140–150/90–100 — not lower, because the placenta is perfused by the mother's pressure), <strong>prevent fits with magnesium sulphate</strong>, and <strong>plan the birth</strong> — after stabilisation, by induction or caesarean, at once if she is 34 weeks or more or if the mother or fetus is deteriorating at any gestation, with dexamethasone for lung maturity if between 24 and 34 weeks and the condition allows a 24–48 hour delay. <strong>Fluids are restricted</strong> to about 80 mL an hour in total (including the magnesium infusion) and charted strictly, because the leaky capillaries and the kidney make pulmonary oedema the way these women die from fluid; urine output is measured hourly with a catheter. Observations: blood pressure every 15 minutes until stable then every 30 minutes, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation, reflexes, urine output, fetal heart, and the symptoms — headache, vision, epigastric pain — asked about every time.",
          ],
